\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $X$ be a finite set and let $\mathcal F\subseteq2^X$ be a nonempty family of distinct subsets whose union is nonempty. It is union-closed if $A\cup B\in\mathcal F$ for every $A,B\in\mathcal F$. For $x\in X$, write $f_{\mathcal F}(x)=\#\{A\in\mathcal F:x\in A\}$. The family $\{\varnothing\}$ is excluded, as required in the standard formulation.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every such union-closed family, is there an element $x\in\bigcup\mathcal F$ such that
\[
f_{\mathcal F}(x)\geq\tfrac12|\mathcal F|?
\] \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.07022}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must some element occur in at least half the sets of any finite union-closed family with a nonempty set?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1321 (COMB-003). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Abigail Raz, \emph{Note on the union-closed sets conjecture}, arXiv:1704.07022v1 (2017), abstract and Section 1. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.07022}.
\end{description}
